Three choices I made that you should know about
Thinner anodize than I assumed earlier. I used 0.05 mm when sizing backlash. For teeth this small, 0.025 mm is the better callout because thick hard coat chips on fine edges. It leaves you slightly more backlash than planned, which is harmless.
Bores masked. Anodize grows about half its thickness on each surface, so an unmasked 6.00 mm bore would shrink by about 0.025 mm and may not fit the shaft. If your anodizer prefers not to mask, cut the bores oversize by that amount instead.
7075 anodizes less well than 6061. The coating comes out a little softer and less uniform. It is still the right choice here because the tooth cores need 7075’s strength.

When you call the anodizer, ask for PTFE hard coat by name. Shops sell it as Nituff, Tufram or Hardtuf.